# Normalise everything to LF in the repository, whatever the working tree
# holds — the source is built on Linux CI and read on every platform. The
# specific rules below override this catch-all, so they come after it: in
# gitattributes the last matching pattern wins.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are read by a shell inside a Linux container, and a shebang
# ending in a carriage return fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M".
#
# The repository stores them with LF, so CI is fine either way. The trap is
# local: with core.autocrlf on, a fresh checkout on Windows writes CRLF into
# the working tree, and `docker build` copies the working tree rather than the
# index. The image then builds cleanly and dies on first start, which is a long
# way from the cause.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same argument for the files a container reads as an entrypoint by another
# name. `mvnw` is one: the Dockerfile copies it in and runs it with `/bin/sh`,
# so a carriage return in its shebang builds an image that dies on first start.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
mvnw text eol=lf

# And its counterpart stays a Windows batch file.
mvnw.cmd text eol=crlf
